Du Fu was a prominent Chinese poet of the Tang dynasty, known for his significant contributions to Chinese and Japanese literature. Alongside Li Bai, he is considered one of the greatest Chinese poets. His life was deeply affected by the An Lushan Rebellion, which caused widespread devastation and personal hardship. Du Fu's poetry often reflected his moral concerns and historical observations, earning him the titles "Poet-Historian" and "Poet-Sage." His works, which number nearly 1,500 poems, have been preserved and continue to influence literary culture.

